CogniPrep practises aptitude tests. There are 56 assessment providers on the site and 369 games behind them, and the way that number grows is in rounds: pick a handful of batteries candidates actually sit, research each one properly, then build.
The round we merged yesterday started with six. It shipped four, and 25 new games with them. The other two produced a research dossier each and no game code at all, which was the correct outcome in both cases and the more interesting half of the round.
The one that no longer exists
The CFAT is the Canadian Forces Aptitude Test. It is well known, it is heavily searched, and prep sites still sell practice for it.
The Canadian Armed Forces removed it from Regular Force selection on 31 October 2024. It was replaced by the Scored Employment Application Form, a scored questionnaire about your life so far, plus a 30 minute personality inventory called the TSD-PI. Four independent sources agree: the Department of National Defence's own release, the Supplementary Estimates, an Auditor General report, and the live forces.ca joining page, which has quietly deactivated its CFAT help articles.
Neither replacement is an ability test. There is nothing to drill. So the build was cancelled and the research turned into three things:
- An explainer post at /blogs/cfat-replaced-by-seaf that answers the search instead of selling against it.
- One row in our "which test am I taking" lookup table, routing CFAT searches to that post with the label replaced by the SEAF in October 2024. Not practised here.
- Past tense corrections everywhere our own copy still described the CFAT as current, including a neighbouring provider's hub page and its research dossier.
The thing I would not have predicted is how much of that needed locking down. A page whose whole point is "we do not sell this" is one careless edit away from becoming a page that does, so it has its own test file:
it('offers no practice game, because nothing we sell practises the replacements', () => {
  expect(post.relatedGames ?? []).toEqual([]);
});
it('is not a provider and has no hub', () => {
  expect(ALL_PROVIDERS as readonly string[]).not.toContain('cfat');
  expect(existsSync(join(process.cwd(), 'app', 'games', 'cfat'))).toBe(false);
});
it('has no Canadian Armed Forces employer entry, since the association ended', () => {
  expect(EMPLOYER_ASSESSMENTS.some((p) => /canadian armed forces/i.test(p.name))).toBe(false);
});
Every post on the site can carry related games. This one asserts that it carries none. The remaining cases pin the facts the post is built on: that it cites DND, that it names the TSD-PI, that it says "Neither one is an ability test", and that it says "Nothing official settles this" about the one question nobody has answered, which is whether any Reserve or career transfer route still sets the CFAT.
A test asserting that a sentence of marketing copy exists looks odd until you have watched a hedge get edited out for reading badly.
The one that already existed under another name
The second cancellation was the Australian Defence Force. The ADF sets one aptitude test at its YOU Session, now called the Job Opportunities Assessment.
The research came back with 51 questions in 20 minutes, candidate instructions that read nearly word for word like a vendor's own, and example questions matching that vendor's published screenshots. The vendor is Revelian, whose RCAT we already built.
Our rule for that case is older than this round: an organisation that sits candidates on a covered vendor's standard test gets an employer page, not a provider of its own. Building an adf provider would have meant a second copy of a test we already have, with a different logo on it.
So the ADF became /employers/australian-defence-force, with the association recorded at medium confidence rather than high, because the match is inferred from identical published content and the ADF has never named a vendor. That confidence level is not decoration; it decides the wording. The page says the JOA "matches the Revelian RCAT" and never says the ADF uses Revelian, because we do not know the second thing.
The useful side effect is that research aimed at a provider we did not build turned out to be evidence about one we had. The ADF publishes more about this test than its vendor does, and six concrete details in that material were things our existing RCAT either got wrong or hedged, including a line on the practice page that still called a countdown unconfirmed when the ADF guide confirms it. Those landed as corrections to the test we already sell.
See it
Four checks, all on public pages, no account needed:
- Open the CFAT explainer and search the page for Nothing official settles this. Then look for a link to practice material. There is none, and a test keeps it that way.
- Open which assessment am I taking and search for SEAF. That row is the retired test pattern: the honest label sits in the row, and the link goes to an article. 153 rows on that page, of which 147 point at a provider, 4 at the Australian Defence Force page and exactly 1 at an explainer.
- Open the ADF employer page and search for matches the Revelian RCAT, which is there, then for uses Revelian, which is not. That is the medium confidence rule rendered as English.
- Then try cogniprep.app/games/cfat, the hub that was never built. It answers 200 with the title "Game Not Found", because a catch-all route under /games takes any slug it does not recognise. It is absent from all 375 URLs in our sitemap, so nothing we publish points at it, but a soft 404 is still a soft 404 and it is now on the list.
Two of six tracks ending in a dossier and no code is not waste. One of them would have been practice for a test that no longer exists, and the other would have been a second copy of a test we already had.

The UK train driver battery has a reporting model that is awkward for a practice app.
Each test is passed or failed on its own. RSSB recommends a national minimum for each, some train companies set a higher "enhanced" standard of their own, and there is no single enhanced standard. The industry standard that governs the process sets no pass marks, and none are published anywhere. The candidate gets an outcome and never a score, and every result goes on a national database that every train company can check.
So we built six of those tests, and every one of them ends on a screen that has to say something about how you did. Our scorers produce numbers. The real test produces a word.
One metric, three bands, and a sentence about where they came from
The compromise is a band. A sitting is classified as below the national standard, inside its range, or inside the range of an enhanced standard, stored as a single standardBand metric of 0, 1 or 2 so the feedback layer can read it without a new persisted field.
Every threshold behind those bands is ours. For three of the six tests, Group Bourdon and the two Trainability for Rules and Procedures tests, they are anchored on something real: candidates post their marks and their outcomes on railway forums, often with the year and the company. Each threshold in the module carries a comment naming the accounts it rests on, so a future edit has to argue with a source rather than with a number.
The feedback then says exactly that:
The real test gives you no score. You pass or fail it on its own, against either RSSB's recommended national minimum or a company's stricter enhanced standard, and every result goes on a national database that every train company checks. Pass marks are not published, so the band below comes from what candidates report passing with.
That sentence shipped on all six tests. Then the audit of the generated feedback asked the obvious question about the other three.
Nobody reports a mark for half of them
The WAFV, the TEA-Occ and the ATAVT are vigilance, attention and perception tests sat on a dedicated test system. Candidates write about them constantly. What they write is what the test felt like, how fast the tones came, how long the half hour was. Nobody posts a number, because the system does not show them one.
So for those three, "the band below comes from what candidates report passing with" was false. Not a hedge that was slightly too strong, and not a rounding of someone else's figure: a citation of evidence that does not exist.
The fix is a set and a second copy of every sentence that makes the claim:
/**
 * The tests whose bands rest on no candidate report at all. Their frame and
 * band sentence say the band is our estimate, because "candidates report"
 * would be untrue.
 */
export const TD_ESTIMATED_GAMES: ReadonlySet<string> = new Set(['td-wafv', 'td-tea-occ', 'td-atavt']);
export const TD_BAND_PHRASE: Record<TdBand, string> = {
  0: 'below what candidates report passing with',
  1: 'in the range candidates report for the national standard',
  2: 'in the range candidates report for an enhanced standard',
};
export const TD_BAND_PHRASE_ESTIMATED: Record<TdBand, string> = {
  0: 'below our estimate of the national standard',
  1: 'in our estimated range for the national standard',
  2: 'in our estimated range for an enhanced standard',
};
Not a boolean parameter threaded through the feedback functions, and not a conditional inside one string. Two complete sets of words, picked by id. The band logic, the thresholds and the stored metric did not change at all; only the provenance claim did.
The same substitution had to land in four places that each render it differently: the result feedback, the instruction screen each of those three games shows before you start, the provider hub page, and the article about the battery. A per-test detail line was wrong too, because it compared a count against "the range candidates report passing with" rather than against our own estimated range.
The test that enforces it in both directions
The interesting assertion is not that the three estimated tests say "our own estimate". It is that the other three must never say it:
it.each(IDS)('%s only credits candidate reports where some exist', (id) => {
  for (const level of ['weak', 'middling', 'strong'] as const) {
    const report = generateGameInsights(id, PERCENTILE[level], SITTINGS[id][level]).join(' ');
    const category = level === 'weak' ? 'low' : level === 'middling' ? 'average' : 'high';
    const feedback = generateClientFeedback('s', id, PERCENTILE[level], category, SITTINGS[id][level]);
    const text = [report, ...feedback.strengths, ...feedback.weaknesses].join(' ');
    if (TD_ESTIMATED_GAMES.has(id)) {
      expect(text, `${id} ${level}`).not.toMatch(/candidates report (passing|for)/);
      expect(text, `${id} ${level}`).toContain('our own estimate');
    } else {
      expect(text, `${id} ${level}`).not.toMatch(/our (own )?estimate/);
    }
  }
});
Both directions matter. A test that only checked for the weaker wording would pass if we gave up and called all six bands a guess, which would throw away three tests' worth of real evidence to make one rule simpler. The feedback is generated per sitting from synthetic weak, middling and strong states run through the real scorer, so the assertion covers the text a user actually sees rather than the template it came from.
See it
No account needed for either of these.
- Open the train driver hub and search the page for our own estimate. The sentence names the three tests it applies to, and the sentence before it says the other three come from dated candidate accounts. Both are in the same paragraph, under a heading that says our bands are not pass marks.
- Open the train driver cheating guide and search for candidates report passing with. The clause continues "or against our own estimate for the three tests where nobody reports a mark", which is the whole distinction in one sentence.
If you are counting occurrences in the HTML, every sentence on these pages appears twice, once in the markup and once in the serialised component payload. That is a different post.
A practice product is a chain of claims about somebody else's test. The thresholds are the easy part. Saying out loud which ones are inherited from evidence and which ones we made up is the part that changes what a user does with the number.

Before letting 256-colour PNGs into an image pipeline, I wanted to understand one failure. A sticker with a soft drop shadow goes through colour reduction, and on a white page the shadow comes back as a solid black band with a knife-cut edge. My test file is a pink ribbon sticker from Wikimedia Commons (by K B, CC BY 2.0), a third-party image, and the figures only show its top loop. 17.9% of its pixels are semi-transparent. 256 colours were not the problem. An indexed PNG can store soft alpha fine, and the result depends on how many distinct alpha values the encoder writes into one small chunk.
Colour type 3 keeps alpha in its own chunk
The original is colour type 6, four bytes per pixel with alpha stored next to RGB. A 256-colour PNG is colour type 3. Each pixel is an index into PLTE, which holds up to 256 RGB entries of three bytes. Transparency lives in the optional tRNS chunk, one alpha byte per palette entry in the same order, and entries past its end count as opaque. How many distinct values those bytes hold is up to the encoder. I wrote a small reader in Node with no image library:
function paletteAlpha(bytes) {
  const view = new DataView(bytes.buffer, bytes.byteOffset, bytes.byteLength);
  const report = { colourType: null, paletteSize: 0, alphaBytes: 0 };
  let alpha = [];
  for (let at = 8; at < bytes.length; ) {
    const size = view.getUint32(at);
    const tag = String.fromCharCode(...bytes.subarray(at + 4, at + 8));
    const payload = bytes.subarray(at + 8, at + 8 + size);
    if (tag === 'IHDR') report.colourType = payload[9];
    if (tag === 'PLTE') report.paletteSize = size / 3;
    if (tag === 'tRNS') { report.alphaBytes = size; alpha = [...payload]; }
    at += size + 12;
  }
  while (alpha.length < report.paletteSize) alpha.push(255);
  const steps = [...new Set(alpha)].sort((a, b) => a - b);
  report.belowOpaque = alpha.filter((a) => a < 255).length;
  report.steps = steps.length;
  report.widestGap = Math.max(0, ...steps.slice(1).map((a, i) => a - steps[i]));
  return report;
}
It walks the chunks, pads the alpha list to palette size per the spec, and reports entries below 255, distinct alpha values and the widest gap between neighbouring values. Count distinct values, not entries. My 98-level file has 129 entries below 255 but only 98 distinct values, because several colours share one alpha. Output for six versions of the sticker:
ribbon.png               type=6 tRNS=0B below255=0 steps=0 gap=0
ribbon-2.png             type=3 tRNS=256B below255=1 steps=2 gap=255
ribbon-10.png            type=3 tRNS=256B below255=9 steps=10 gap=43
ribbon-34.png            type=3 tRNS=256B below255=33 steps=34 gap=18
ribbon-98.png            type=3 tRNS=256B below255=129 steps=98 gap=12
ribbon-png8-export.png   type=3 tRNS=256B below255=1 steps=2 gap=255
The two-step file is the black band. Its tRNS is a full 256 bytes, yet the only entry below 255 is the fully transparent colour, so every pixel at alpha 128 or above went opaque and the rest vanished. The 10, 34 and 98 level files come from a quick reducer I wrote for comparison, not from pngquant or any product. The last line is the PNG-8 export from ImgIng's image compressor at its defaults, run on 2026-09-30, and it has the same two steps. Its PNG compression page says transparent PNG-8 keeps multi-level alpha. My file did not, and I am reporting bytes, not guessing why.
Browsers read alpha per palette entry
A format feature only helps if decoders honour it. I decoded the 34-level file in the Chromium 149 open-source build, Firefox 151 and WebKit 26.5 (the engine, not a Safari release) and read the pixels back from a canvas. All three reported 34 alpha levels and matched Pillow's decode on semi-transparent pixel count and alpha sum.
This is a Noto Color Emoji outline (Apache 2.0) at 4x on blue. The original has 17 alpha levels and only 1,548 semi-transparent pixels. With two steps the anti-aliased rim becomes a staircase, and with 20 steps it looks like the original.
Fewer alpha steps show up as rings
A shadow is a smooth ramp, and every gap between neighbouring alpha values becomes a visible ring. Error here is the mean RGB difference (0 to 255) from the original over semi-transparent pixels, both composited on white. Two steps gave 44.17, 10 steps 8.23, 34 steps 3.48 and 98 steps 1.75. In the same encoder the 34-level file was 121,346 bytes against 111,178 for two steps, only 9.1% more. At 10 steps, with a widest gap of 43, the shadow shows clear contour lines. At 34 steps (gap 18) they thin out, and at 98 steps (gap 12) the shadow is close to the original.
Dithering adds grain but no alpha steps
With two steps, dithering never touches alpha. ImgIng's exports with dithering on and off had identical alpha and the same white error (44.07 and 44.06), but the dithered file was 1.59 times larger, 148,242 bytes against 93,317. On my 34-level file, Floyd-Steinberg diffusion over colour and alpha did break the rings into grain. White error still rose from 3.48 to 4.29 and the file grew 65%. Opaque pixels pushed to semi-transparent went from 573 to 1,850, seen as white specks on the pink edge.
What I check before shipping an indexed PNG
I run the reader first. A type 3 file with one tRNS entry below 255 has lost every soft pixel, however fine it looks on a dark page. Then I look on white and on a colour, never only on black, since the two-step ribbon scored 2.33 on black against 44.17 on white. For assets with large soft shadows I skip PNG-8. For the ribbon I used ImgIng (imging.ai) to convert to WebP at its default quality 84, and it went from 430.7 KB to 98.8 KB with alpha identical to the original pixel for pixel. The conversion ran in the browser and the export sent no non-GET requests.
This covers two samples. Coloured shadows, glows, Chrome stable, Safari, display pipelines and chat apps that recompress images are untested. Before switching a batch of PNGs to 256 colours, run a tRNS count over the exports and reject any file whose source had soft pixels but whose output has just two alpha steps.

The CSS border-image property is one of those features we take for granted. And it’s not new at all! In fact, Andy Clarke has a fairly recent article that “revisits” the property and shows just how creative we can get with it. Go read that article — it’s worth it.
The TL;DR is that we can use an image or gradient as a border instead of standard (ahem, boring) styles, say, solid or dashed border lines. What I want to do is take them a little further to show how we can animate border images for even more interesting user interfaces!
Like this:
Why border images at all?
There’s a pretty big caveat to border images: they don’t curve to border shapes. That’s a big limitation for an animation that travels around an element like we’re doing here. But we can work around that for this example.
But that also begs the question: “Why work around that if there are other approaches?” Just look at Temani Afif’s approach that uses a CSS mask (he has a great write-up on it):
I like border-image because it’s super efficient, especially when it comes to animating border images and gradients. Mainly because it’s easy to replicate the designs across all the borders — it’s automatic — and also because we can “slice” the images using the border-image-slice property. It’s similar to background-size and background-position where a single slice of the image can be used. However the border image slices can run across the entire borders, and animating it creates beautiful effects, as you’ll see.
The HTML
We’re doing nothing crazy here. Just a div that contains a bit of formatted text in it:
<div class=card><strong>Bruce Wayne</strong></div>
What about the image? That’s set up as a CSS background, so let’s jump right in that.
Base styles
We’re giving the .card set dimensions then dropping in a background image on it:
.card {
  width: 150px;
  aspect-ratio: 0.69;
  position: relative;
  background: center/90% no-repeat;
  background-image: url("batman.jpg");
}
Cool, cool. I’m also adding a little styling to the text to make sure it’s not right on top of the background:
The border image
I’ll be using a single-color CSS gradient because (1) it’s a type of background image and (2) it’s a little easier to demonstrate the animation.
I’ll also set all the values of border-image separately in their own longhand properties, so it’s clearer for you to know which value affects the gradient in which way.
Let’s start with the effect of a simple linear-gradient:
.card {
  /* ... */
  /* Creates a linear color */
  border-image-source: linear-gradient(-45deg, red 0%, transparent 0%);

  /* Controls how the gradient is carved */
  border-image-slice: 1;
  /* Sets the physical thickness of the border */
  border-image-width: 5px;
}
You may have noticed in the demo that there is a little gap between the animated border and the image. We can push the border away from the .card using the border-image-outset property:
.card {
  /* ... */
  border-image-source: linear-gradient(-45deg, red 0%, transparent 0%);
  border-image-slice: 1;
  border-image-width: 5px;
  /* Pushes the border away from the div */
  border-image-outset: 5px;
}
We need border-image-source and border-image-width to render the border. Initially, the image source — the gradient — is fully transparent. Because both the red and transparent colors are set to start at 0%, the browser doesn’t have any space to blend the colors, and since transparent comes second in the code, it gets the starting position at 0% and fills the rest of the gradient. We’re using border-image-slice: 1 to create 1px slices of the gradient, which creates a smooth fill of the gradient across the whole border.
Animating the border image
There’s something else to remember about gradients, specifically about animating them: we can’t by default. Transitioning (or interpolating) from one color to another is difficult because we’re essentially transitioning the starting and ending points of a gradient, which are percentages.
That’s where registering our own custom property comes into play because we can indeed animate CSS variables:
@property --p {
  syntax: "<percentage>";
  initial-value: 0%;
  inherits: false;
}
And we can use that in our gradient:
.card {
  /* ... */
  border-image-source: linear-gradient(-45deg, red var(--p), transparent 0%);
}
Now let’s say that we want --p to transition from its default value of 0% to 100% when the .card is hovered:
.card {
  /* ... */
  border-image-source: linear-gradient(-45deg, red var(--p), transparent 0%);
  /* ... */
  &:hover {
    --p: 100%;
  }
}
This tells the browser that red should finish its transition at the 100% mark (the very end). The gradient will grow to be a solid red color, like the border is drawing itself:
Variations
We could have stopped with that last example, but there’s so much else we can do here! We can change direction, make it faster or slower, stop in other positions, or even use different types of gradients. Seriously, it’s a lot of fun.
Let’s try using a conic-gradient instead of a linear one. We can animate the border-image-slice value, and use border-image-repeat: round to tile the slices within the borders. The round tiling tries to fit a whole number of tiles in the border and if it couldn’t, the image slightly stretches or squeezes until the fit occurs.
.card {
  /* Creates a sharp, expanding color wheel using the custom angle variable */
  border-image-source: conic-gradient(from 0deg, red 0deg, transparent 0%);
  /* Repeats the sliced regions smoothly without clipping */
  border-image-repeat: round;
  border-image-width: 5px;
  border-image-slice: 1;
}
We still need to register custom properties to move the gradient around, this time to transition the border-image-slice value (we’ll call it --n) and the conic-gradient’s angle (which we’ll call --a):
Note: You can also animate border-image-slice directly and not through a registered custom property. The result will slightly vary based on how the browser handles those changes.
/* Registers a custom property for the border-image-slice */
@property --n {
  syntax: "<number>";
  initial-value: 1;
  inherits: false;
}
/* Registers a custom property for the gradient angle */
@property --a {
  syntax: "<angle>";
  initial-value: 0deg;
  inherits: false;
}
Let’s drop those into our original CSS:
.card {
  border-image-source: conic-gradient(from var(--a), red var(--a), transparent 0%);
  border-image-width: 5px;
  border-image-slice: var(--n);
  border-image-repeat: round;
}
…and tell the CSS exactly which properties we’re transitioning, which is good for performance:
.card {
  border-image-source: conic-gradient(from var(--a), red var(--a), transparent 0%);
  border-image-width: 5px;
  border-image-slice: var(--n);
  border-image-repeat: round;
  transition-property: --n, --a;
  transition-duration: 0.6s;
}
…and then make the properties transition on hover:
.card {
  border-image-source: conic-gradient(from var(--a), red var(--a), transparent 0%);
  border-image-width: 5px;
  border-image-slice: var(--n);
  border-image-repeat: round;
  transition-property: --n,--a;
  transition-duration: 0.6s;
  &:hover {
    /* Increases slicing depth to repeat/tile the border pattern */
    --n: 20;
    /* Rotates the gradient full circle to draw the border in */
    --a: 360deg;
  }
}
The slice value (--n) is initially 1. It goes up to 20 on hover. That means the slice gets bigger (up to 20px thick). You’ll notice this when you see the breaks in the borders.
More examples!
Like I said earlier, there’s so much we can do with animated border images. We can animate multiple colors, use repeating gradients. Here are the examples I pulled together.
Your homework: pick these apart — and please let me know if you make any other effects! I’d love to see them.

A new Vercel Sandbox is now ready to run commands and read or write files when creation completes.
Sandbox.create() now resolves only after snapshot restoration completes, so the first command or file operation no longer waits for it. Startup errors also reject Sandbox.create() instead of surfacing during that operation.
import { Sandbox } from '@vercel/sandbox';
// Resolves after snapshot restoration. Startup failures reject here.const sandbox = await Sandbox.create();
// Runs without waiting for snapshot restoration.await sandbox.runCommand('node', ['--version']);
Run a command right after creating a sandbox.
Snapshot restoration time now counts toward creation instead of the first operation. Creation is about 50 ms longer at p50, with a larger increase possible for uncached snapshots. The total time through the first operation remains the same.
This change applies automatically, with no SDK upgrade or configuration changes required. See the Sandbox.create() documentation for details.

